# Reference for .env.spike-opendesk, the connection file of the throwaway measurement
# topology of phase 17 (compose.spike-opendesk.yml, openDesk spike).
#
# Read this file, never copy it. `cp .env.spike-opendesk.example .env.spike-opendesk` would
# hand the bootstrap a secret that is published in this repository, and APP_SECRET is bearer
# equivalent: whoever knows it can impersonate every account of the instance. Every secret
# line below is therefore commented out, and the bootstrap refuses any value that is not 64
# lower case hex characters (CR-02).
#
# The real .env.spike-opendesk is written by scripts/bootstrap_spike_opendesk.sh and is
# git-ignored, because it carries two working app passwords, the HaRP shared key and the app
# secret of the registration. This example only names the variables.
#
#   export HP_SHARED_KEY="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
#   export SECRET_KEY_BASE="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
#   docker compose -f compose.spike-opendesk.yml up -d --wait
#   bash scripts/bootstrap_spike_opendesk.sh
#   set -a && . ./.env.spike-opendesk && set +a
#
# Both exports are needed for every command of that topology, `ps` included, and not only
# for the services that use them: compose interpolates the whole file before it filters by
# profile (measured 2026-08-28, see the header of compose.spike-opendesk.yml).

NC_MCP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8091

# The ExApp behind the reverse proxy. Everything the app serves lives under this base.
NC_MCP_EXAPP_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8091/exapps/mcp_connector

NC_MCP_TEST_USER=alice
NC_MCP_TEST_APP_PASSWORD=xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx

# Second, restricted user. The two-account negative proof of D-05 is mandatory on both
# access ways, so this one is not optional in this phase.
NC_MCP_TEST_USER2=bob
NC_MCP_TEST_APP_PASSWORD2=xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx

# The read-only share and the two Talk conversations the integration tests of the repository
# expect. The bootstrap creates them because it is a copy of scripts/bootstrap_exapp.sh; no
# measurement of this phase reads them.
NC_MCP_TEST_SHARED_DIR=mcp-share-0123456789
NC_MCP_TEST_SHARED_FILE=mcp-share-0123456789/mcp-shared-file-0123456789.md
NC_MCP_TEST_PRIVATE_FILE=mcp-private-0123456789.md
NC_MCP_TEST_SHARED_MARKER=mcp-shared-file-0123456789
NC_MCP_TEST_TALK_ROOM=MCP-Talk-offen-Grüße-aus-Hamburg
NC_MCP_TEST_TALK_READONLY_ROOM=MCP-Talk-nurlesen-Straße-ohne-Ausgang

# The registration identity. APP_SECRET is fixed on purpose: a re-registration hands the
# container a new secret otherwise, and a process that still holds the old one answers 401
# to everything (02-RESEARCH.md, pitfall 11). The bootstrap generates it with
# `openssl rand -hex 32` and writes it into .env.spike-opendesk; the line stays commented
# out here so that no copy of this file can ever carry a working one.
APP_ID=mcp_connector
# APP_SECRET=<64 lower case hex characters, openssl rand -hex 32>
APP_VERSION=0.1.0

# Shared between the HaRP container and the daemon registration in Nextcloud. Export it
# before `docker compose up`, the compose file has no default.
# HP_SHARED_KEY=<64 lower case hex characters, openssl rand -hex 32>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage B, OpenProject (profile op, plan 17-03 and following)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Rails derives every session cookie and every signed message from SECRET_KEY_BASE, so the
# compose file carries no default for it either (WR-11). Export it before any command of
# that topology, see the note at the top.
# SECRET_KEY_BASE=<64 lower case hex characters, openssl rand -hex 32>

# The administrator of the fresh OpenProject instance. The all-in-one image seeds the
# account `admin` with the password `admin` and forces a change at the first sign in; the
# password that replaces it is a throwaway value of an instance on loopback and belongs
# here, never into a commit.
# OP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<throwaway password of the OpenProject admin account>

# The two OpenProject accounts of the two-account negative proof (D-05): user B must not be
# able to see the work package of user A, on both access ways.
# OP_USER_A_PASSWORD=<throwaway password of OpenProject user A>
# OP_USER_B_PASSWORD=<throwaway password of OpenProject user B>

# An OpenProject API token of user A. Only for reading the API shape of section 3 of the
# report, never for a measurement of the user identity: a token of one account cannot show
# whether an access way carries the identity of the calling user.
# OP_API_TOKEN=<OpenProject API token, created under My account, Access tokens>

# The OAuth application of way 1 (D-04), created inside OpenProject under
# Administration, Authentication, OAuth applications. The client secret is shown exactly
# once at creation time.
# OP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=<client id of the OAuth application in OpenProject>
# OP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=<client secret of the OAuth application in OpenProject>

# The OAuth pair of way 0: the credentials integration_openproject in Nextcloud is
# configured with. A second, separate application in OpenProject, not the one above, so a
# measurement can tell the two ways apart in the OpenProject log.
# NC_OP_CLIENT_ID=<client id integration_openproject is configured with>
# NC_OP_CLIENT_SECRET=<client secret integration_openproject is configured with>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage C, Keycloak and user_oidc (profile oidc, plan 17-07, S5 of D-07)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The bootstrap administrator of the Keycloak container. The compose file sets neither of the
# two: a required variable there would be demanded by every command of the topology, stage A
# included. Hand them in when the profile is started:
#   KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin \
#   KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=... \
#     docker compose -f compose.spike-opendesk.yml --profile oidc up -d
# KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<throwaway password of the Keycloak admin account>

# The secret of the Nextcloud client in the Keycloak realm, read by user_oidc.
# KC_CLIENT_SECRET=<client secret of the Nextcloud client in Keycloak>
